":"Taken with iPhone 5|People of Santa Clara, California|Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR|Flickr images missing SDC source of file|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|People with mobile phones in California|Files from Intel Free Press Flickr stream|Flickr images missing SDC Flickr photo ID|Flickr images missing SDC published in|Culture of Santa Clara, California","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"37.385500","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"-121.967000","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"Lloyd Lee-Lim and his 4-year-old son Ben have been on a technology-aided scavenger hunt to find giant heart statues located throughout the San Francisco Bay Area.\n<p>See more:\nFather-Son Scavenger Hunt Takes Them Inside Intel\nSmartphone 'Picture of the Day' project combs the San Francisco Bay Area in search of heart statues from public art project.\n</p><p>What started as a straightforward hunt for a mother's Valentine's Day gift has grown into a months-long father and son quest to photograph more than 170 giant hearts from a public art project. Finding the 5-foot tall heart statues isn't always easy -- many are now in private hands -- but the pair recently tracked down one of the biggest single collections of the artwork at Intel headquarters in Santa Clara, Calif.\n</p>\nLloyd Lee-Lim and his 4-year-old son Ben have been on a technology-aided scavenger hunt of sorts that has taken them all over the San Francisco Bay Area.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2013-05-10 10:52:12","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/intelfreepress/9020722046/sizes/o/in/photostream/\">https://www.flickr.com/photos/intelfreepress/9020722046/